             <span class="problem-title">R1 · A test and its assumptions</span>
             <p>State a test of whether the dispersion of window two differs from that of window one. Name the statistic, name every assumption it needs, and say which of those assumptions Lab 1 checks.</p>
             <details class="solution"><summary>Solution</summary>
-              <p>A natural statistic is the ratio of the two sample variances, \(s_2^{2} / s_1^{2}\), with the window-two variance in the numerator. A null distribution for this ratio depends on assumptions about the changes, such as their distribution and their independence across months. This lesson does not derive that null distribution, and no p-value is reported here. Lab 1 checks none of these assumptions. It checks the telescoping identity, and Part A's exact-zero result; Part B only counts the exact zeros. A test that reports a p-value needs the null distribution derived or cited from a source admitted to the reference list first.</p>
+              <p>Let \(s_1\) and \(s_2\) be the dispersions of window one and window two from Definition 1.1. A natural statistic is the ratio of the two sample variances, \(s_2^{2} / s_1^{2}\), with the window-two variance in the numerator. A null distribution, meaning the distribution of this ratio when the two windows have the same dispersion, depends on assumptions about the changes, such as their distribution and their independence across months. This lesson does not derive that null distribution, and no p-value (the probability, under the null distribution, of a result at least as extreme as the one observed) is reported here. Lab 1 checks none of these assumptions. It checks the telescoping identity, and Part A's exact-zero result; Part B only counts the exact zeros. A test that reports a p-value needs the null distribution derived or cited from a source admitted to the reference list first.</p>
             </details>
