           <dt>Burden change</dt>
           <dd>The burden change from period 0 to period \(t\) is \(100\,(P_0 / P_t - 1)\), in percent of the period-0 real value. A positive number is a rise in the real burden. Because \(V_t / V_0 = P_0 / P_t\), the change needs no rounded real value.</dd>
           <dt>Monthly log change and dispersion</dt>
-          <dd>For a monthly exchange rate \(S_m\), the monthly log change is \(\ln(S_m / S_{m-1})\), per month and not annualised. The dispersion of a window is the sample standard deviation of its monthly log changes, with denominator \(n - 1\), reported in percent per month. Each standard deviation is computed by the two-pass formula (the mean, then the squared deviations from it). An exact zero change is a month whose rate equals the previous month's rate, so its log change is zero.</dd>
+          <dd>For a monthly exchange rate \(S_m\), the monthly log change is \(\ln(S_m / S_{m-1})\), per month and not annualised. The dispersion of a window (defined under The three windows, below) is the sample standard deviation of its monthly log changes, with denominator \(n - 1\), where \(n\) is the number of changes in the window, reported in percent per month. Each standard deviation is computed by the two-pass formula (the mean, then the squared deviations from it). An exact zero change is a month whose rate equals the previous month's rate, so its log change is zero.</dd>
           <dt>The three windows</dt>
           <dd>The windows are for the GBP column that Lab 3 reads, which is the US dollar price of one pound sterling. Window one holds every monthly change of that column, 1971-02 to 2026-09 (668 changes). Window two holds the changes dated 2000-01 to 2026-09 (321 changes); its first change uses the rate of 1999-12. Windows one and two are the windows of Lesson 4. Window three holds the changes dated 2025-10 to 2026-09 (12 changes), the twelve months to the last month of the embedded snapshot (defined under <a href="#data">the data section</a>), and is the current window of the brief (section <a href="#brief">The brief</a>, below).</dd>
